The following original example merges half-open intervals. An interval (a, b) contains positions from a up to, but not including, b. Empty intervals are discarded; reversed intervals are rejected. Adjacent intervals are merged because there is no uncovered position between them.
```python
def merge_intervals(intervals):
    cleaned = []
    for start, end in intervals:
        if start > end:
            raise ValueError('reversed interval')
        if start != end:
            cleaned.append((start, end))
    cleaned.sort()
    result = []
    for start, end in cleaned:
        if not result or start > result[-1][1]:
            result.append([start, end])
        else:
            result[-1][1] = max(result[-1][1], end)
    return [tuple(item) for item in result]

assert merge_intervals([(7, 9), (1, 4), (3, 7)]) == [(1, 9)]
assert merge_intervals([(2, 2), (5, 6)]) == [(5, 6)]
assert merge_intervals([]) == []
```
The useful invariant is that `result` is sorted and contains no overlapping or adjacent intervals. Before a new interval is considered, every earlier input interval has already been represented in that result. Sorting ensures that the new interval cannot extend to the left of the last result interval's start. It can only begin after the last interval, overlap it, or touch its endpoint. Therefore the algorithm needs to inspect only the final result interval rather than searching the entire list.
The choice to merge adjacency is a policy, not an unavoidable feature of interval arithmetic. Replacing `start > result[-1][1]` with `start >= result[-1][1]` would preserve adjacent intervals as separate entries. A test suite should state which interpretation is intended. It should also include nested intervals, duplicate intervals, negative coordinates, and intervals arriving in reverse order. None of those cases requires a separate algorithm, but each can expose an implementation that accidentally relies on the examples being unusually tidy.

A dependency graph can be ordered without relying on the insertion order of a dictionary. This version uses a heap so that, among all currently available nodes, the lexicographically smallest name is chosen first. The ordering is deterministic for a fixed graph.
```python
import heapq

def dependency_order(nodes, edges):
    outgoing = {node: set() for node in nodes}
    degree = {node: 0 for node in nodes}
    for before, after in edges:
        if before not in outgoing or after not in outgoing:
            raise ValueError('edge refers to an unknown node')
        if after not in outgoing[before]:
            outgoing[before].add(after)
            degree[after] += 1
    ready = [node for node, count in degree.items() if count == 0]
    heapq.heapify(ready)
    ordered = []
    while ready:
        node = heapq.heappop(ready)
        ordered.append(node)
        for successor in outgoing[node]:
            degree[successor] -= 1
            if degree[successor] == 0:
                heapq.heappush(ready, successor)
    if len(ordered) != len(outgoing):
        raise ValueError('dependency cycle detected')
    return ordered
```
The sets prevent a repeated edge from increasing the incoming degree twice. Without that protection, a graph described by a file containing duplicate rows could appear to contain a cycle even when its distinct edges were acyclic. The final length check detects a cycle because nodes remaining inside a cycle never reach an incoming degree of zero. Nodes depending on that cycle may also remain unprocessed, so the residual set is not necessarily the cycle itself.
This function returns one valid ordering, not every possible ordering. If two tasks are independent, their relative positions are chosen by the heap's tie-breaking rule. Changing that rule can change the output without changing correctness. Consumers should decide whether they need any valid ordering, a reproducible ordering, or a scheduling policy that considers duration and resource limits. Those are related but different requirements. Reproducibility is useful for testing; it does not by itself make the resulting schedule efficient.

This small C++ buffer has a fixed capacity and refuses insertion when full. It is intentionally single-threaded. The presence of a circular index does not make a data structure safe for concurrent producers or consumers.
```cpp
#include <array>
#include <cstddef>
#include <optional>
#include <utility>

template <typename T, std::size_t Capacity>
class Ring {
    static_assert(Capacity > 0);
    std::array<std::optional<T>, Capacity> slots_;
    std::size_t head_ = 0;
    std::size_t size_ = 0;
public:
    bool push(T value) {
        if (size_ == Capacity) return false;
        const auto tail = (head_ + size_) % Capacity;
        slots_[tail] = std::move(value);
        ++size_;
        return true;
    }
    std::optional<T> pop() {
        if (size_ == 0) return std::nullopt;
        auto value = std::move(slots_[head_]);
        slots_[head_].reset();
        head_ = (head_ + 1) % Capacity;
        --size_;
        return value;
    }
    std::size_t size() const { return size_; }
};
```
The logical tail is derived from the head and current size. The implementation never needs to shift existing elements when removing the oldest one. Resetting the vacated optional destroys the stored object rather than merely declaring its slot unused. That distinction matters when `T` owns memory or another resource.
There are several legitimate policies for a full buffer. It can reject new input, overwrite the oldest item, block the caller, or return an error object describing the rejected item. This example selects rejection and communicates it through a boolean. A caller that ignores the boolean can still lose data, so the surrounding program must account for that result.
Tests should fill the buffer, remove several items, wrap around the physical end, and fill it again. They should confirm logical order rather than assuming that live objects occupy a single contiguous range. Concurrency would require an additional design covering ownership, publication, and memory ordering; adding a mutex or atomics without specifying those rules would not be a complete explanation.

A delimiter checker must decide whether punctuation inside quoted text participates in nesting. The example below treats single and double quotes as string delimiters and allows a backslash to escape the following character within a string. It is a teaching parser, not a complete parser for Python, JavaScript, or another language.
```python
def balanced(text):
    opening = {'(': ')', '[': ']', '{': '}'}
    closing = set(opening.values())
    stack = []
    quote = None
    escaped = False
    for character in text:
        if quote is not None:
            if escaped:
                escaped = False
            elif character == '\\':
                escaped = True
            elif character == quote:
                quote = None
            continue
        if ord(character) in (34, 39):
            quote = character
        elif character in opening:
            stack.append(opening[character])
        elif character in closing:
            if not stack or stack.pop() != character:
                return False
    return quote is None and not stack
```
The stack stores expected closing characters rather than opening characters. Seeing a closing delimiter therefore becomes a direct comparison with the most recent expectation. The parser keeps quotation state separate from delimiter state. While a quoted string is active, brackets are ordinary characters and do not change the stack.
This simplified grammar has deliberate limits. It does not recognise comments, multiline quotation rules, interpolation, or language-specific escape sequences. A string ending with an escape character remains unterminated, and the final return rejects it. Those choices should be described alongside the function rather than discovered accidentally by users.
A valuable test set includes an unmatched opening bracket, an unexpected closing bracket, nested different delimiters, a quoted closing bracket, an escaped quote, and an empty input. Merely testing several balanced examples cannot show that the function rejects malformed cases. Parsing tests are strongest when they examine transitions between states, because many mistakes occur at the moment a character changes its role.

An enum can represent states that require different information. In this original Rust example, a document is either a draft containing editable text, a submitted document with a revision identifier, or a published document with both an identifier and a public path. The representation prevents a draft from accidentally claiming to have a publication path.
```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
enum Document {
    Draft { text: String },
    Submitted { revision: u64, text: String },
    Published { revision: u64, path: String },
}

fn submit(document: Document, revision: u64) -> Result<Document, Document> {
    match document {
        Document::Draft { text } => {
            Ok(Document::Submitted { revision, text })
        }
        other => Err(other),
    }
}

fn publish(document: Document, path: String) -> Result<Document, Document> {
    match document {
        Document::Submitted { revision, .. } => {
            Ok(Document::Published { revision, path })
        }
        other => Err(other),
    }
}
```
Both transition functions consume the old value. On success they return a value representing the new state. On failure they return the original document instead of discarding it. The type alone does not validate the revision identifier or the public path; those checks would belong in the transition logic or in dedicated validated types. The example illustrates state representation rather than a complete publication service.
A design using several independent boolean flags might allow contradictory combinations such as draft=true and published=true. An enum replaces that collection of flags with one explicit choice. It also makes code handling the states visible through pattern matching. Adding a new variant forces a review of matches that need to remain exhaustive.
The broader lesson is not that every object needs a state machine. It is that fields whose meanings depend on each other should be represented in a way that makes valid combinations easy to construct and invalid combinations difficult to express.

This asynchronous mapper runs at most `limit` operations at a time and places results in input order. Completion order may differ from input order, so workers write to the position associated with each claimed item.
```javascript
async function mapLimited(values, limit, operation) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 1) {
    throw new RangeError('limit must be a positive integer');
  }
  const results = new Array(values.length);
  let next = 0;
  async function worker() {
    while (next < values.length) {
      const index = next;
      next += 1;
      results[index] = await operation(values[index], index);
    }
  }
  const count = Math.min(limit, values.length);
  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: count }, () => worker()));
  return results;
}

const lengths = await mapLimited(
  ['cedar', 'elm', 'willow'],
  2,
  async word => word.length
);
```
The index is claimed before the `await`. In JavaScript's ordinary single-threaded execution of this function, another worker cannot interleave between reading and incrementing `next`, because no asynchronous suspension occurs there. This observation does not generalise to shared memory accessed by actual threads. The concurrency here comes from overlapping asynchronous operations.
The function's failure semantics deserve attention. `Promise.all` rejects when one worker rejects, but it does not automatically cancel operations already started by other workers. Those operations may still produce side effects. A production caller must decide whether to wait for them, provide an abort signal, collect individual failures, or use an operation that is safe to retry. Returning results in input order also does not make side effects occur in that order.
Useful tests track the maximum number of simultaneously active operations, introduce different delays, and inject a rejection after several items have started. Empty input should create no workers and return an empty result. The numerical concurrency limit is only one part of the contract; ordering and failure behaviour are equally important.

A matrix view describes how to locate elements without owning the underlying allocation. This C++ sketch stores a pointer, a row count, a column count, and a stride measured in elements. The stride can exceed the visible column count when rows include padding.
```cpp
#include <cstddef>
#include <stdexcept>

struct MatrixView {
    float* data;
    std::size_t rows;
    std::size_t columns;
    std::size_t row_stride;

    float& at(std::size_t row, std::size_t column) const {
        if (row >= rows || column >= columns) {
            throw std::out_of_range("matrix index");
        }
        return data[row * row_stride + column];
    }

    MatrixView subrows(std::size_t first, std::size_t count) const {
        if (first > rows || count > rows - first) {
            throw std::out_of_range("row interval");
        }
        return {data + first * row_stride, count, columns, row_stride};
    }
};
```
The subtraction-based interval check avoids forming `first + count` before validating the range. With unsigned integers, an overflowing sum could wrap around and make an invalid interval appear small. This is a local illustration of a broader rule: checking a computed result is not enough if the computation itself can overflow.
The view does not keep the allocation alive. A caller must ensure that the storage outlives every view and that the dimensions and stride describe valid memory. The bounds checks protect the logical row and column indices, but they cannot prove that an arbitrary pointer refers to a sufficiently large allocation. Ownership and indexing are separate responsibilities.
The `const` qualifier on `at` means that calling it does not change the view's descriptor. It does not make the referenced elements immutable, because the descriptor holds a mutable `float*` and returns `float&`. An immutable view would use a pointer to const elements. Distinguishing constness of a handle from constness of its target prevents a common source of confusion when designing lightweight interfaces.

A result type can make expected failure part of an interface instead of representing every outcome with either a value or an exception. The following TypeScript example distinguishes a successful lookup, a missing entry, and malformed input.
```typescript
type LookupResult =
  | { kind: 'found'; name: string; position: number }
  | { kind: 'missing'; query: string }
  | { kind: 'invalid'; reason: string };

function lookup(names: readonly string[], query: string): LookupResult {
  const trimmed = query.trim();
  if (trimmed.length === 0) {
    return { kind: 'invalid', reason: 'query is empty' };
  }
  const position = names.indexOf(trimmed);
  if (position < 0) {
    return { kind: 'missing', query: trimmed };
  }
  return { kind: 'found', name: names[position], position };
}

function describe(result: LookupResult): string {
  switch (result.kind) {
    case 'found': return `${result.name} is at ${result.position}`;
    case 'missing': return `No entry for ${result.query}`;
    case 'invalid': return `Cannot search: ${result.reason}`;
  }
}
```
The `kind` field is a discriminator. Once a branch has checked that field, the other fields available in the branch follow from the selected variant. A missing result does not contain a meaningless position of minus one, and an invalid result does not pretend to contain a searched query.
This does not imply that exceptions should never be used. Unexpected failures, such as an unavailable external resource, may belong to a different error channel. The important distinction is between outcomes the caller is expected to handle routinely and failures that prevent the operation from meeting its contract.
The example also normalises whitespace before searching. That is a visible policy: a name with significant leading or trailing spaces would not be searchable exactly as stored. Case folding and Unicode normalisation would introduce further decisions. A compact function can still embody several assumptions, and good interface documentation identifies the assumptions that affect observable results.

A function can return a view into an existing string without allocating a new string. The lifetime annotation in this Rust example connects the returned slice to the borrowed input. It does not extend the lifetime of the underlying storage.
```rust
fn first_field<'a>(line: &'a str) -> &'a str {
    match line.find(',') {
        Some(position) => &line[..position],
        None => line,
    }
}

fn main() {
    let record = String::from("north,17,open");
    let field = first_field(&record);
    assert_eq!(field, "north");
    println!("{}", field);
}
```
The slice refers to bytes owned by `record`. Rust's borrowing rules prevent code from destroying or mutating that storage in ways that would invalidate the active reference. The explicit lifetime here makes the relationship visible, although this particular signature can also be written using lifetime elision.
String indexing deserves a separate explanation. A byte position is not automatically a valid character boundary. In this example, `find(',')` returns the byte position of an ASCII comma, which is a valid boundary in the UTF-8 string. Replacing that position with an arbitrary integer could make the slicing operation invalid. A function intended to select a number of characters would need to traverse character boundaries rather than assume that each character occupies one byte.
Returning a borrowed view can avoid copying, but it also couples the result's lifetime to the source. An owning return value such as `String` would be more independent at the cost of an allocation and a copy. The appropriate choice depends on how the result will be used. Performance is not simply a contest to eliminate allocations; interfaces must also express ownership clearly enough that callers can use them safely.
